[2026-01-30T15:05:07.880Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lzk3TPxhyoNmhvVgZ7DqAk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Implementation: Extract declarative report builder to eliminate 80% duplicated report generation code\n     2→\n     3→## Context Area\n     4→Research Templates\n     5→\n     6→## Description\n     7→Extract declarative report builder to eliminate 80% duplicated report generation code\n     8→\n     9→## Implementation Details\n    10→\n    11→## Vision\n    12→Extract common report generation logic into a declarative report builder that eliminates ~80% of duplicated code across 11 template files while maintaining full customization capability.\n    13→\n    14→## Business Value\n    15→- Reduces template file sizes by 60-80% (current templates are 250-580 lines each)\n    16→- New templates can be created in 50 lines instead of 400+\n    17→- Bug fixes and improvements apply to all templates automatically\n    18→- Easier to add new report variants (currently requires ~100 lines per variant)\n    19→\n    20→## Scope\n    21→**Included:**\n    22→- Create ReportBuilder base class with declarative section definitions\n    23→- Extract common section rendering patterns (findings lists, extracted data formatting, markdown generation)\n    24→- Implement section registry with configurable rendering strategies\n    25→- Migrate one template as proof of concept\n    26→\n    27→**Excluded:**\n    28→- Template configuration (FindingTypeConfig, TemplateConfig) - already well abstracted\n    29→- Perspective and verification systems\n    30→- Frontend/API changes\n    31→\n    32→## Key Components\n    33→1. `ReportBuilder` class - Declarative report section definitions with rendering pipeline\n    34→2. `SectionRenderer` protocol - Interface for custom section rendering (findings list, metrics table, timeline)\n    35→3. `MarkdownBuilder` utility - Common markdown patterns (headers, tables, bullet lists with prefixes)\n    36→4. Template migration - Update one template to use new system, validate output parity\n    37→\n    38→## Technical Approach\n    39→1. Analyze common patterns across generate_*_report() methods\n    40→2. Create declarative section specification with title, finding_types, max_items, extracted_fields\n    41→3. Implement ReportBuilder with render_section method\n    42→4. Templates define sections declaratively instead of imperative code\n    43→\n    44→## Files to Modify\n    45→- `actor/src/templates/base.py` - Add ReportBuilder, SectionSpec, MarkdownBuilder classes\n    46→- `actor/src/templates/due_diligence.py` - Migrate as first template (260 lines to ~80 lines)\n    47→- All other templates after validation\n    48→\n    49→## Success Criteria\n    50→- [ ] Due diligence template migrated with identical output\n    51→- [ ] Code reduction of 60%+ in migrated template\n    52→- [ ] New report variant can be added with <20 lines of declarative config\n    53→- [ ] No regression in report quality or format\n    54→\n    55→## Potential Challenges\n    56→- Extracted data schemas vary between templates - solved by optional field mapping\n    57→- Some templates have unique formatting needs - solved by allowing custom renderer override\n    58→- Output parity verification - create test cases comparing old vs new output\n    59→\n    60→## Instructions\n    61→\n    62→1. Read and understand the implementation details above\n    63→2. Identify all files that need to be modified\n    64→3. Implement the changes following the guidance provided\n    65→4. Ensure code quality and type safety\n    66→5. Test the changes work as expected\n    67→\n    68→## Notes\n    69→\n    70→This requirement was generated from an accepted Development Direction.\n    71→Focus on implementing exactly what is described above.\n    72→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"063d9692-0974-4418-8890-3933b0ef315a","uuid":"e87569b2-6d03-4ad6-a1d1-5293ac816605","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\mkdol\\dolla\\res\\.claude\\commands\\dir-1769783008317-extract-declarative-report-builder-to.md","content":"# Implementation: Extract declarative report builder to eliminate 80% duplicated report generation code\n\n## Context Area\nResearch Templates\n\n## Description\nExtract declarative report builder to eliminate 80% duplicated report generation code\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n## Vision\nExtract common report generation logic into a declarative report builder that eliminates ~80% of duplicated code across 11 template files while maintaining full customization capability.\n\n## Business Value\n- Reduces template file sizes by 60-80% (current templates are 250-580 lines each)\n- New templates can be created in 50 lines instead of 400+\n- Bug fixes and improvements apply to all templates automatically\n- Easier to add new report variants (currently requires ~100 lines per variant)\n\n## Scope\n**Included:**\n- Create ReportBuilder base class with declarative section definitions\n- Extract common section rendering patterns (findings lists, extracted data formatting, markdown generation)\n- Implement section registry with configurable rendering strategies\n- Migrate one template as proof of concept\n\n**Excluded:**\n- Template configuration (FindingTypeConfig, TemplateConfig) - already well abstracted\n- Perspective and verification systems\n- Frontend/API changes\n\n## Key Components\n1. `ReportBuilder` class - Declarative report section definitions with rendering pipeline\n2. `SectionRenderer` protocol - Interface for custom section rendering (findings list, metrics table, timeline)\n3. `MarkdownBuilder` utility - Common markdown patterns (headers, tables, bullet lists with prefixes)\n4. Template migration - Update one template to use new system, validate output parity\n\n## Technical Approach\n1. Analyze common patterns across generate_*_report() methods\n2. Create declarative section specification with title, finding_types, max_items, extracted_fields\n3. Implement ReportBuilder with render_section method\n4. Templates define sections declaratively instead of imperative code\n\n## Files to Modify\n- `actor/src/templates/base.py` - Add ReportBuilder, SectionSpec, MarkdownBuilder classes\n- `actor/src/templates/due_diligence.py` - Migrate as first template (260 lines to ~80 lines)\n- All other templates after validation\n\n## Success Criteria\n- [ ] Due diligence template migrated with identical output\n- [ ] Code reduction of 60%+ in migrated template\n- [ ] New report variant can be added with <20 lines of declarative config\n- [ ] No regression in report quality or format\n\n## Potential Challenges\n- Extracted data schemas vary between templates - solved by optional field mapping\n- Some templates have unique formatting needs - solved by allowing custom renderer override\n- Output parity verification - create test cases comparing old vs new output\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Read and understand the implementation details above\n2. Identify all files that need to be modified\n3. Implement the changes following the guidance provided\n4. Ensure code quality and type safety\n5. Test the changes work as expected\n\n## Notes\n\nThis requirement was generated from an accepted Development Direction.\nFocus on implementing exactly what is described above.\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
